Design: We will balance skin‐tone representation in training by using a WeightedRandomSampler with sample weights inversely proportional to skin‐tone frequency, ensuring each batch contains more examples from underrepresented skin tones. This oversampling strategy replaces uniform random sampling in the train DataLoader, which should improve fairness without altering the model architecture or loss function. We keep the same DenseNet121 backbone with partial freezing and weighted BCE loss, perform 5‐fold CV to report mean AUROC, then retrain on the full dataset using the sampler and save the final model. A predict(folder) function loads the saved model and returns malignancy probabilities for new images.
